{"id": 126999, "acmi_id": "", "title": "The Ferals", "title_zh_hans": "", "title_pa": "", "title_hi": "", "title_annotation": "", "title_annotation_zh_hans": "", "title_annotation_pa": "", "title_annotation_hi": "", "slug": "the-ferals", "creator_credit": "", "credit_line": "", "credit_line_zh_hans": "", "credit_line_pa": "", "credit_line_hi": "", "headline_credit": "", "video_links": [], "record_type": "group", "type": "Film", "is_on_display": false, "last_on_display_place": null, "last_on_display_date": null, "is_context_indigenous": false, "material_description": "", "unpublished": false, "go_live_at": null, "external": false, "first_production_date": null, "commissioned": false, "public_domain": false, "external_references": [], "brief_description": "<p>The Ferals (1994–95) brought grunge energy and dark humour to Australian children’s television. Full of slang, 90s pop culture and backyard chaos, it followed four feral animals squatting in a shed: Rattus the rat, Modigliana the cat, Mixy the rabbit and Derryn the dog.</p>\n<p>Designer Gareth Frost and puppet maker Tina Matthews drew on punk aesthetics to create hand and rod puppets that were lightweight but sturdy enough for cartoon violence. </p>\n<p>Puppeteers gave each character distinct personalities, blending unruly human and animal traits. Modigliana, or “Moddie”, was a stroppy diva often compared to Miss Piggy. Mixy’s erratic comedy carried a darker joke: myxomatosis, introduced to Australia to cull feral rabbits. The Ferals made children’s puppetry scruffy and unmistakably Australian.</p>", "constellations_primary": [], "constellations_other": [], "recommendations": [], "creator_credit_for_label": "", "creator_credit_for_label_zh_hans": "", "creator_credit_for_label_pa": "", "creator_credit_for_label_hi": "", "headline_credit_for_label": "", "headline_credit_for_label_zh_hans": "", "headline_credit_for_label_pa": "", "headline_credit_for_label_hi": "", "title_for_label_zh_hans": "", "title_for_label_pa"